Three key problems in rendering, shadow resolution, motion blur estimation, and object matting, are addressed by a new method that differentiates effectively. This method, based on a pre-trained neural network, analyzes an image and identifies specific pixels that represent shading. Through optimization, this information is used to refine a scene model accurately, resulting in improved solutions for the mentioned problems. Additionally, the method enables dealing with scenes that contain volumetric effects, addressing a limitation in previous studies. Extensive testing on benchmark datasets reveals that the method outperforms current state-of-the-art techniques in shadow resolution and motion blur estimation, while producing comparable or better results in object matting. The method also facilitates solving other inverse problems, such as joint motion and object tracking, and depth estimation, as demonstrated by the experiments. Furthermore, the method allows for adjusting parameters in rendering, opening up new possibilities in computer vision. The code for this method has been made available publicly. 

